---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import { CRITERIA } from '../lib/score.mjs';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site';
---
<Page title="How We Score Products – The Best Tripod Score" description="How the Best Tripod Score works: five criteria (stability, portability, height, build, value), their weights, the formula and what each number means." updated="October 15, 2026">
  <p>Every product we recommend gets a <strong>Best Tripod Spec Score</strong> out of 5. It answers one question: <em>how well does this support hold your camera steady, for the way you actually shoot and travel?</em> It's our own editorial rating, built from manufacturer specifications, user manuals and the research listed in each guide. It is not a customer rating, and we never copy star ratings from stores.</p>

  <h2>The five criteria</h2>
  <p>We rate each product from 1 to 10 on five criteria, weighted by how much they matter to a photographer or video shooter:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Criterion</th><th scope="col">Weight</th><th scope="col">What a 10 looks like</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {CRITERIA.map((c) => (
        <tr>
          <td>{c.label}</td>
          <td>{Math.round(c.weight * 100)}%</td>
          <td>{{
            stability: 'Rock steady with its intended camera and lens, with a rated load well above that gear, no flex at full height, and a hook or spikes for wind.',
            portability: 'Light for its class and short when folded: fits a carry-on or straps to a daypack without a second thought.',
            height: 'Reaches eye level without raising the center column, and still goes low enough for ground-level shots.',
            build: 'Quality materials, quick and secure leg locks, a smooth head that locks without drift, and an Arca-Swiss compatible plate.',
            value: 'Fair price for what it does, with nothing important you need to buy separately to use it well.',
          }[c.key]}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>The formula</h2>
  <p><strong>Score = (Stability × 30% + Portability × 20% + Height × 15% + Build × 20% + Value × 15%) ÷ 2</strong>, rounded to one decimal.</p>
  <p>Some criteria don't apply to every product. A quick-release plate, a super clamp or a tripod bag has no working height, so we leave that criterion out and rescale the other weights so they still add up to 100%. That keeps accessories from being punished for something they were never meant to do.</p>

  <h2>How we pick each number</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Stability:</strong> 10 rated load at least three times your camera and lens, solid at full height, hook and spikes · 8 comfortable with a mirrorless kit and a mid-size zoom · 6 fine for light cameras, wobbles with long lenses or in wind · 4 or lower phone and compact cameras only.</li>
    <li><strong>Portability (weight and folded length):</strong> 10 carry-on friendly and among the lightest in its class · 8 straps to a daypack all day · 6 car-to-location only · 4 or lower a studio or vehicle tripod.</li>
    <li><strong>Working height:</strong> 10 eye level with the center column down plus a low-angle option · 8 eye level only with the column partly raised · 6 chest height · 4 or lower tabletop height (judged against its category, so a mini tripod is scored as a mini tripod).</li>
    <li><strong>Build (materials, leg locks, head):</strong> 10 carbon fiber or premium alloy, fast reliable locks, a smooth head with independent pan and Arca-Swiss clamp · 8 solid aluminum with good locks and a capable head · 6 plastic parts in load-bearing places or a head that drifts · 4 or lower known reliability problems.</li>
    <li><strong>Value:</strong> 10 hard to beat for the money and nothing essential to add · 8 good value · 6 premium price, or a head or plate you will likely need to replace.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What each score band means</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>4.5 to 5.0:</strong> outstanding for its job. Buy it with confidence if it fits your camera, lens and budget.</li>
    <li><strong>4.0 to 4.4:</strong> very good, with one clear trade-off (a little heavy, a little short, or a basic head).</li>
    <li><strong>3.5 to 3.9:</strong> good for a specific use, such as light travel kits or phone video, but limited outside it.</li>
    <li><strong>Below 3.5:</strong> we rarely recommend it, and only when it is the one option for an unusual need.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What the score is not</h2>
  <p>It is a spec-based score, not a measurement: we haven't had these products in hand, so each criterion is judged from published figures (for example load rating, materials, lock type, size and weight) and from owner reports, never from our own tests. It isn't a lab test or a customer review average, and it doesn't change with price swings or sales. When a product is revised or replaced, we re-check it and update the score. Read more about our <a href="/how-we-research/">research process</a>, the <a href="/tripod-buying-checklist/">{SITE.name} buying checklist</a> and our <a href="/editorial-policy/">editorial policy</a>.</p>
</Page>
